.docname {Package CLI}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

`@bakingrl/create-plugin` provides two commands:

- `create-bakingrl-plugin` scaffolds trusted v4 packages.
- `bakingrl-plugin` validates, inspects, packs, signs, and locally installs
  trusted v4 packages.

## Templates

```sh
create-bakingrl-plugin my-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-native --template native-sidecar-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-platform --template platform-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-contributor --template contributor-plugin
create-bakingrl-plugin my-content --template content-pack-plugin
```

Available templates:

- `extension-plugin`: extension host entry plus a webview contribution.
- `native-sidecar-plugin`: extension host entry plus a Rust JSON-RPC stdio
  sidecar.
- `platform-plugin`: extension host service, webview, public resource,
  and extension point for other packages.
- `contributor-plugin`: dependency-backed contribution with a service,
  and public resource.
- `content-pack-plugin`: resource-only package that contributes public JSON and
  SVG content to another package.

Generated packages declare `schemaVersion: "bakingrl.plugin/4"`, and
`bakingrlApi: "2.3.0"`. They include an explicit empty permission declaration
and an author-owned `marketplace/listing.json` ready to edit.

## Validate

```sh
bakingrl-plugin validate .
```

Validation checks:

- manifest schema, id, name, version, `bakingrlApi` compatibility, runtime,
  permissions, dependencies, contributes, and host-owned settings;
- exact or single-terminal-wildcard bus, registry, and storage patterns;
- structured HTTP, WebSocket, and listen endpoints;
- relative storage paths without URI prefixes, absolute paths, `..`, or
  backslashes;
- built JavaScript entries for extension host and declared webviews;
- a package-local `package.json` with `type: "module"` whenever
  `runtime.node` is declared;
- complete `surface` webviews with immutable capability declarations;
- sidecar command paths and `jsonrpc-stdio` declarations;
- sidecar health-check declarations when present;
- package-local paths for declared contribution schemas, resources, webviews,
  and runtime entries;
- dependency-backed cross-package contributions;
- local references from extension points and contributions to declared
  services and resources;
- absence of bundled Node runtime binaries such as `node`, `node.exe`, or
  `bin/node*`.

Current validation accepts `contributes.extensionPoints`,
`contributes.contributions`, `contributes.resources`, and
`contributes.webviews`. A contribution targeting another package must declare a
matching top-level dependency. Runtime API validation accepts manifests in the
`2.3.x` window, with `2.3.0` as the minimum, and rejects every other target.

`doctor`, `pack`, and `install-local` add a static Node runtime preflight. They
copy only bundle-eligible files to an isolated staging directory, syntax-check
every JavaScript module without evaluating it, and follow static imports,
export-from declarations, and literal dynamic imports. The entry must expose
`activate(context)`. Node built-ins are accepted, while external bare imports
are rejected because release packages do not contain `node_modules/`. JSON
modules require `with { type: "json" }`, and non-literal dynamic imports are
rejected because their installable target cannot be proven. Import attributes
are accepted only for JSON and cannot contain extra keys; package-local module
specifiers cannot contain query strings or fragments. The preflight never
imports the plugin or runs its top-level code.

Installable package trees cannot contain symbolic links, including a symbolic
link used as the package root, manifest, package metadata, runtime entry, or
asset. Reserved source directories such as `.git/`, `node_modules/`, and
`dist-bundles/` are excluded case-insensitively so Windows path casing cannot
make them part of an artifact. All remaining file and directory paths must be
Windows-portable and unique under portable Unicode/case normalization;
case-only collisions and non-canonical spellings of package control artifacts
are rejected. The same extraction limits as the host apply before packaging:
at most 25 MiB per file and 150 MiB uncompressed for the package.

## Doctor

```sh
bakingrl-plugin doctor .
```

`doctor` prints a JSON summary of `bakingrlApi`, permissions, dependencies,
sidecars, contribution ids, resources, webviews, build entries, and the staged
Node runtime preflight.

## Inspect

```sh
bakingrl-plugin inspect .
```

`inspect` prints the normalized v4 manifest surface used by the host:
`schemaVersion`, `bakingrlApi`, `permissions`, `runtime`, and `contributes`.

## Author Listing

Every generated package includes `marketplace/listing.json`. Validate its
package id, text limits, repository, media URLs, screenshots, and links with:

```sh
bakingrl-plugin validate-listing .
```

The listing is display metadata owned by the package author. It is not a trust
decision and it is not the signed marketplace catalogue.

## Pack

```sh
npm run build
bakingrl-plugin pack .
```

`pack` validates and preflights one staged snapshot, then computes
`manifest.hashes.json`, the optional signature, and the `.brlp` from that same
snapshot. Source changes made concurrently after staging cannot alter the
bundle being written to `dist-bundles/`. The final bundle is replaced
atomically only after generated source artifacts have been published; a failed
replacement rolls those artifacts back. If an unsigned pack retains an
existing signature, that signature must still verify against the new staged
hashes or the command asks you to re-sign or delete it.

Signing key files are validated as an Ed25519 keypair: the declared public key
must be the one derived from the private PEM before any hashes or signature are
published.

To sign while packing:

```sh
bakingrl-plugin keygen bakingrl-signing-key.json
bakingrl-plugin pack . --sign bakingrl-signing-key.json
```

Do not commit signing keys, signatures, hashes, bundles, or generated build
outputs unless a release process explicitly requires them.

## Prepare A Submission

After producing a signed release bundle, emit review input with:

```sh
bakingrl-plugin prepare-submission . \
  --developer example \
  --bundle-url https://github.com/example/package/releases/download/v1.0.0/package.brlp \
  --listing-url https://raw.githubusercontent.com/example/package/v1.0.0/marketplace/listing.json \
  --platform windows-x64 \
  --output submission.json
```

The `bakingrl.marketplace-submission/1` document records the package version,
Runtime API, author listing location, artifact hash, signing public key, and
declared permissions. Before emitting it, the CLI reads the exact bundle once,
verifies its ZIP structure, complete file hashes, Ed25519 signature, manifest,
and listing against the staged package snapshot, and derives the reported SHA
and public key from those verified bytes. It deliberately contains no review
status, approval, or `bakingrl.marketplace/2` catalogue structure; those belong
to the marketplace repository.

## Local Install

```sh
bakingrl-plugin install-local .
```

This stages the same bundle-eligible files used by `pack`, runs the Node runtime
preflight, then replaces the package in the BakingRL app data directory. The
source `.git/`, `node_modules/`, and `dist-bundles/` directories, `.env` files,
and standard private signing-key filenames are never copied. Public package
artifacts such as `manifest.hashes.json` and `signature.ed25519` remain eligible.
Use it after `npm run build` when testing a package in the host app.
